&lt;b&gt;project.&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:03.039 --> 00:11:07.399
&lt;b&gt;But I remember the woman from the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;campsite,&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:07.399 --> 00:11:13.520
&lt;b&gt;so happy to make you coffee at 5 or 5:30&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;a.m.&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:14.039 --> 00:11:16.079
&lt;b&gt;That's what I find most beautiful.&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:16.319 --> 00:11:20.640
&lt;b&gt;The energy it gives the people around you.&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:22.159 --> 00:11:26.279
&lt;b&gt;And while we were cycling and chatting,&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:29.039
&lt;b&gt;there were people working by the roadside.&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.600
&lt;b&gt;Their eyes lit up when they saw you on&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;your project.&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:33.439 --> 00:11:36.159
&lt;b&gt;I think that's another thing you&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.960
&lt;b&gt;can pass on through your projects.&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:43.840
&lt;b&gt;When I get to join a project like&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:47.840
&lt;b&gt;that, I'm so happy. It's an incredible&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;privilege.&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:50.720
&lt;b&gt;Then you can say, "I was with Kiki. We did&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;this together."&lt;/b&gt;

00:11:51.960 --> 00:12:01.079
&lt;b&gt;But we really need to find you a&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;publicist!&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:07.279
&lt;b&gt;You're the most down-to-earth guy in the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;world.&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:07.279 --> 00:12:12.079
&lt;b&gt;People would say, "What's he doing on that&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;MBK bike?"&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:12.199 --> 00:12:15.319
&lt;b&gt;And you do these things... Last year,&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:18.800
&lt;b&gt;on that same theme of the Alpine 4,000ers,&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:25.800
&lt;b&gt;you finished a project that took twenty&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;years: skiing all those summits.&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:29.479
&lt;b&gt;You're the only person who can&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:32.560
&lt;b&gt;do something like that. I followed it...&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:34.520
&lt;b&gt;You might find other skiers&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:39.079
&lt;b&gt;with the technical and mountaineering&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;ability,&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:43.439
&lt;b&gt;but then there's the creativity and&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:46.720
&lt;b&gt;patience to go when winter conditions&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;allow.&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:53.079
&lt;b&gt;It's a project that requires so many&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;qualities, and so much patience.&lt;/b&gt;

00:12:54.439 --> 00:13:03.039
&lt;b&gt;I think a lot of that comes from the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;element itself: snow.&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:03.039 --> 00:13:09.600
&lt;b&gt;Snow is alive. It changes every day.&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:15.159
&lt;b&gt;With all the time we spend on snow, we see&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;it changing constantly.&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:21.920
&lt;b&gt;It forces us to be patient and very humble&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;about our decisions.&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:31.079
&lt;b&gt;Yes, skiing all the 4,000ers took time.&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:37.000
&lt;b&gt;But it was fascinating. For years,&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:37.239 --> 00:13:42.119
&lt;b&gt;I'd simply followed whatever caught my&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;eye.&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:42.319 --> 00:13:44.880
&lt;b&gt;I'd see something and think, "That looks&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;good. Let's go there."&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:52.279
&lt;b&gt;This time, for the first time, I had a set&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;list to follow.&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:52.479 --> 00:13:55.600
&lt;b&gt;There was a framework:&lt;/b&gt;

00:13:56.279 --> 00:14:00.159
&lt;b&gt;Pointe Louis-Amédée, the Brouillard Ridge,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Mont Brouillard...&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:00.159 --> 00:14:03.920
&lt;b&gt;Some summits had never been skied,&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.560
&lt;b&gt;and we had to find a route through them.&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:13.680
&lt;b&gt;That was fascinating, and really difficult&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;towards&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:18.399
&lt;b&gt;the end. Finding partners was hard too.&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:20.359 --> 00:14:23.399
&lt;b&gt;So yes, it required patience.&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:32.079
&lt;b&gt;But I'm convinced that comes from snow,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;and nature itself.&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:36.880
&lt;b&gt;You really have to step back and put&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:38.279 --> 00:14:45.159
&lt;b&gt;what you do into perspective in that&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;environment.&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:46.079 --> 00:14:51.720
&lt;b&gt;You mentioned partners. That's something I&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;felt on the traverse too.&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.960
&lt;b&gt;Especially on the Brouillard, and the&lt;/b&gt;

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:59.479
&lt;b&gt;traverse of Les Droites towards the Verte.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:01.199 --> 00:15:03.960
&lt;b&gt;In a way, I felt more at&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:10.640
&lt;b&gt;ease being alone, because it was my&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;project.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.479
&lt;b&gt;I didn't have that responsibility.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:20.920
&lt;b&gt;Not that the others weren't strong enough.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:21.439 --> 00:15:25.079
&lt;b&gt;But it's your project, and you're bringing&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;them into it.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:25.079 --> 00:15:27.439
&lt;b&gt;How did you manage that?&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:27.439 --> 00:15:32.319
&lt;b&gt;Some of those summits were serious&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;undertakings.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:32.319 --> 00:15:38.600
&lt;b&gt;That reminds me of Everest. I wasn't there&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:44.039
&lt;b&gt;that day; you'd gone with Jordi and Seb.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:44.039 --> 00:15:45.479
&lt;b&gt;Even then, you could say, "I'll go first.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:46.199 --> 00:15:49.640
&lt;b&gt;It's my project, and I'll take&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;responsibility."&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:53.840
&lt;b&gt;You were taking responsibility for the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;others too.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:57.479
&lt;b&gt;That's a lot to carry.&lt;/b&gt;

00:15:58.359 --> 00:16:03.920
&lt;b&gt;When you're fully committed to something,&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:09.239
&lt;b&gt;you bring people into your projects.&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:15.560
&lt;b&gt;Sometimes they're not there for&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;themselves. They're there to help you.&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:16.399 --> 00:16:19.600
&lt;b&gt;Then, when a decision requires commitment,&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:24.039
&lt;b&gt;they may not want to go on.&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:24.039 --> 00:16:28.880
&lt;b&gt;Those are moments of uncertainty. You&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;start to doubt.&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:31.439
&lt;b&gt;You've invested so much in your project,&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:37.640
&lt;b&gt;and you know everything is there for it to&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;work.&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:38.199 --> 00:16:41.359
&lt;b&gt;But the other person's perspective&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:42.319 --> 00:16:46.920
&lt;b&gt;and behaviour really affect your&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;decisions.&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:53.520
&lt;b&gt;And that's not easy. That's why...&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:54.159 --> 00:16:58.279
&lt;b&gt;Even those incredibly long days you did in&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;the Mont Blanc massif...&lt;/b&gt;

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:06.479
&lt;b&gt;No one could have kept up with you anyway.&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:13.520
&lt;b&gt;But making that commitment with someone&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;else is complicated to manage.&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:14.560
&lt;b&gt;It is.&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:15.159 --> 00:17:21.079
&lt;b&gt;In a climbing partnership, so much&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;communication is non-verbal. We don't talk.&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:25.439
&lt;b&gt;It's funny: we've never really talked&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;about it.&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:25.439 --> 00:17:32.159
&lt;b&gt;Sometimes, when you're alone and&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:32.159 --> 00:17:36.199
&lt;b&gt;have doubts, you turn around.&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:36.439 --> 00:17:40.479
&lt;b&gt;But with a partner, you may have&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:41.039 --> 00:17:45.399
&lt;b&gt;doubts and say nothing, waiting for a&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;decision.&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:47.720
&lt;b&gt;You wait for the other person to&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:52.680
&lt;b&gt;speak, but they're doing the same thing.&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:56.159
&lt;b&gt;And sometimes you get into trouble because&lt;/b&gt;

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:59.800
&lt;b&gt;neither of you dares to say anything.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:00.279 --> 00:18:05.640
&lt;b&gt;It can go both ways. Decision-making is&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;complicated.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:06.119 --> 00:18:09.159
&lt;b&gt;The more people there are, the harder it&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;gets.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:10.039 --> 00:18:13.439
&lt;b&gt;You can end up making worse decisions.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:19.520
&lt;b&gt;With two people, perhaps it's still&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;manageable.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:29.560
&lt;b&gt;It's human nature. That plays a part.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:30.060
&lt;b&gt;Yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:34.119
&lt;b&gt;And you're a father now, too.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:35.420
&lt;b&gt;Yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:36.039 --> 00:18:38.279
&lt;b&gt;It's funny: when we first met,&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:44.199
&lt;b&gt;neither of us had children. Now we both&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;do.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:44.199 --> 00:18:45.399
&lt;b&gt;Yes, time flies.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:50.119
&lt;b&gt;We were talking about this with people:&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:50.119 --> 00:18:56.439
&lt;b&gt;how having children changes the risks we&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;take.&lt;/b&gt;

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:01.600
&lt;b&gt;I thought it hadn't changed anything. That&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;was my impression.&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:03.279 --> 00:19:09.439
&lt;b&gt;But looking back, in recent years I've&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;been&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:09.439 --> 00:19:14.960
&lt;b&gt;more aware of the risks I was taking.&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:23.720
&lt;b&gt;Maybe I thought about them more,&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:26.840
&lt;b&gt;even if I still went ahead in the end.&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:31.800
&lt;b&gt;And with your project, when you start,&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:35.960
&lt;b&gt;the goal is far away. It's easy to stop.&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:37.279 --> 00:19:42.039
&lt;b&gt;But when you have one or two summits left,&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:42.039 --> 00:19:46.199
&lt;b&gt;it's very hard to turn back. That's the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;danger.&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:52.000
&lt;b&gt;And with children... it's complicated.&lt;/b&gt;

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:59.319
&lt;b&gt;It's never black and white.&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:00.279 --> 00:20:02.079
&lt;b&gt;Have you noticed a change?&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:08.199
&lt;b&gt;Like you, at first, not at all.&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:11.840
&lt;b&gt;That was actually when I started skiing my&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:19.119
&lt;b&gt;most technical lines. My eldest was&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;already born.&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:24.640
&lt;b&gt;It didn't change what I did.&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:25.479 --> 00:20:29.520
&lt;b&gt;I started thinking about it&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:33.399
&lt;b&gt;more after my first real&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:33.399 --> 00:20:39.279
&lt;b&gt;mountain mishap, when I felt vulnerable.&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:39.279 --> 00:20:44.520
&lt;b&gt;I'd never really felt... Well, vulnerable,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.960
&lt;b&gt;But when you actually face&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:51.279
&lt;b&gt;real danger and fear,&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:51.279 --> 00:20:58.159
&lt;b&gt;I think it changes your perspective.&lt;/b&gt;

00:20:58.359 --> 00:21:03.960
&lt;b&gt;Maybe it's experience, especially bad&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;experiences,&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:08.680
&lt;b&gt;that makes me think more now.&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:14.319
&lt;b&gt;But when you're fully committed to&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:14.319 --> 00:21:20.680
&lt;b&gt;a project, it's hard to find&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:27.159
&lt;b&gt;a balance and say, "That's enough."&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:34.039
&lt;b&gt;That's what I learned from the 4,000ers&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;project.&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:34.039 --> 00:21:35.039
&lt;b&gt;It was one of the few&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:41.680
&lt;b&gt;times I felt I'd touched elite-level&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;skiing.&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:49.279
&lt;b&gt;I began to understand what performing at&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;that level means.&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:49.279 --> 00:21:54.039
&lt;b&gt;In major competitions, there's such&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:58.600
&lt;b&gt;a fine line between determination&lt;/b&gt;

00:21:58.600 --> 00:22:03.640
&lt;b&gt;and becoming fixated on a goal.&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:07.720
&lt;b&gt;And when you're performing at that level,&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:13.399
&lt;b&gt;your body starts sending signals. The&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;first injuries appear.&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:18.359
&lt;b&gt;I'd never been injured before. I always&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;went so slowly!&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:24.159
&lt;b&gt;But then you get injured, and you&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:24.560 --> 00:22:30.399
&lt;b&gt;have to recover to reach your goals.&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:38.319
&lt;b&gt;I think the line there is very, very fine.&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:39.199 --> 00:22:42.640
&lt;b&gt;It's like the tip of the pyramid.&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:49.039
&lt;b&gt;It's intense, and you have to pass through&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;that point.&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:55.319
&lt;b&gt;We were thinking about this&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:56.039 --> 00:22:59.000
&lt;b&gt;the other day.&lt;/b&gt;

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:04.800
&lt;b&gt;I think our generation was lucky.&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:08.279
&lt;b&gt;We were already young adults when&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:08.279 --> 00:23:16.760
&lt;b&gt;social media arrived. Our childhood was&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;anonymous.&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:18.079 --> 00:23:20.560
&lt;b&gt;When we were learning in the mountains,&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:27.000
&lt;b&gt;we'd look to the people doing these things&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;for inspiration.&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:34.199
&lt;b&gt;The previous generations. We'd read books&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;and&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:34.199 --> 00:23:38.399
&lt;b&gt;the magazines that came out each month.&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:41.000
&lt;b&gt;We didn't have daily updates saying,&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:45.159
&lt;b&gt;"Conditions are good there today. Let's&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;go."&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:49.800
&lt;b&gt;We had to wait and work towards it.&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:57.239
&lt;b&gt;By the time we got there, we'd gained&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;experience and knowledge.&lt;/b&gt;

00:23:58.039 --> 00:24:02.000
&lt;b&gt;Today, it's complicated for young people.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:09.479
&lt;b&gt;You can instantly see on your phone that&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;conditions are good.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:09.479 --> 00:24:13.920
&lt;b&gt;You know exactly what equipment you need.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:13.960 --> 00:24:17.239
&lt;b&gt;We didn't have all that information.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:23.319
&lt;b&gt;Now there's so much information that it's&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:24.039 --> 00:24:30.640
&lt;b&gt;easy to go there without understanding the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;consequences.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:35.199
&lt;b&gt;And I don't know how we can address that.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:40.920
&lt;b&gt;I think it's about how you look at things.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:45.520
&lt;b&gt;Having a way to interpret what you see.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:49.680
&lt;b&gt;Even now, when I look at social media,&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:57.319
&lt;b&gt;I try to look beyond what people want to&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;show me.&lt;/b&gt;

00:24:58.279 --> 00:25:02.399
&lt;b&gt;A young person might see that conditions&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;look great.&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:02.399 --> 00:25:05.600
&lt;b&gt;But that's what they're being shown.&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:10.720
&lt;b&gt;What you need to look at is what's&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:15.920
&lt;b&gt;behind the photo, and what information it&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;gives you.&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:16.399 --> 00:25:19.279
&lt;b&gt;You see someone making a beautiful turn in&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;powder.&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:23.760
&lt;b&gt;That's what they want to show you.&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:24.279 --> 00:25:28.039
&lt;b&gt;But you can look for more information:&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:28.319 --> 00:25:33.439
&lt;b&gt;what is the snow like? What's in the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;background?&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:34.359 --> 00:25:42.159
&lt;b&gt;I've often used old photos and books to&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;scout routes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:42.159 --> 00:25:46.039
&lt;b&gt;I'd look at the skier, but mostly at what&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;was&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:46.319 --> 00:25:50.520
&lt;b&gt;behind them, to write my own story. Or our&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;own story.&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:54.920
&lt;b&gt;So it's about learning how to use social&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;media,&lt;/b&gt;

00:25:55.760 --> 00:26:01.680
&lt;b&gt;and how to look at what we're constantly&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;being shown.&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:02.159 --> 00:26:07.760
&lt;b&gt;That's something we need to teach.&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:08.279 --> 00:26:12.479
&lt;b&gt;As a parent, I ask myself a lot of&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;questions about that.&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:13.399 --> 00:26:18.920
&lt;b&gt;How do we pass on that understanding&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:25.079
&lt;b&gt;and respect for the mountains,&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:26.479 --> 00:26:28.800
&lt;b&gt;and the ability to read them for yourself?&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:32.680
&lt;b&gt;Rather than through someone else's eyes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:34.760
&lt;b&gt;I think brands have a role to play too.&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:45.159
&lt;b&gt;I'll give you a concrete example involving&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;brands.&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:45.159 --> 00:26:52.159
&lt;b&gt;It's marketing, of course. They have&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;products to sell.&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:53.039 --> 00:26:57.600
&lt;b&gt;Take snow and avalanches:&lt;/b&gt;

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:03.039
&lt;b&gt;transceivers, shovels, probes and airbag&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:03.319 --> 00:27:08.680
&lt;b&gt;packs are sold as safety equipment.&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:09.439
&lt;b&gt;Yes, exactly.&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:13.920
&lt;b&gt;But ultimately, that equipment is for&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;rescue.&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:15.560
&lt;b&gt;Rescue, exactly.&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:20.399
&lt;b&gt;What about real safety? People think,&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:22.000
&lt;b&gt;"I'm going off-piste with my shovel,&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:22.319 --> 00:27:26.279
&lt;b&gt;probe and transceiver. I'm equipped, so&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;I'm safe."&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:30.159
&lt;b&gt;But that's not it. That's rescue&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;equipment.&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:34.520
&lt;b&gt;Real safety is saying, "It's snowed a lot.&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:40.680
&lt;b&gt;I'll avoid that steep slope and ski one&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;under 30 degrees."&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:47.560
&lt;b&gt;You behave much more like an animal. I&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;think...&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:48.039 --> 00:27:53.159
&lt;b&gt;Following animal tracks is often a good&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;idea. They often choose a good route.&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:53.439 --> 00:27:56.880
&lt;b&gt;That reminds me of a little story.&lt;/b&gt;

00:27:57.079 --> 00:28:01.520
&lt;b&gt;Something that really stayed with me.&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:01.520 --> 00:28:04.399
&lt;b&gt;When we were in the Himalayas, at Everest,&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:05.199 --> 00:28:09.920
&lt;b&gt;we decided to ski another peak that night.&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:15.520
&lt;b&gt;Conditions on Everest weren't right. There&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;was too much snow.&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:16.239 --> 00:28:17.640
&lt;b&gt;So the two of us set off together.&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:18.479 --> 00:28:25.199
&lt;b&gt;I remember watching you skinning uphill.&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;It was as&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:25.199 --> 00:28:29.119
&lt;b&gt;if your nose was right down in the snow.&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:29.159 --> 00:28:35.640
&lt;b&gt;I really felt I was following an animal,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;moving along like that.&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:36.279 --> 00:28:38.680
&lt;b&gt;I remember thinking how much&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:43.640
&lt;b&gt;you've developed that animal instinct.&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:44.039 --> 00:28:47.640
&lt;b&gt;And you've developed it by spending so&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;much time&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:52.720
&lt;b&gt;alone in the mountains. You can't rely on&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;anyone else.&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:53.119 --> 00:28:56.640
&lt;b&gt;When you cross a slope, you have to&lt;/b&gt;

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:00.319
&lt;b&gt;work out for yourself where it's least&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;dangerous.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:02.279
&lt;b&gt;All the time. Constantly.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:02.279 --> 00:29:05.239
&lt;b&gt;By constantly thinking that way,&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:05.239 --> 00:29:12.359
&lt;b&gt;you develop something incredibly powerful.&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;It's very inspiring.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:18.239
&lt;b&gt;You're relying on your ability to read the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;terrain&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:18.239 --> 00:29:23.359
&lt;b&gt;and on what you feel, rather than on your&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;equipment.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:24.460
&lt;b&gt;Yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:29.399
&lt;b&gt;And I think it's the same with training.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:33.760
&lt;b&gt;People follow the numbers: "This is my&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;heart rate,&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:38.640
&lt;b&gt;my cadence, my pace. I've followed the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;plan."&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:39.119 --> 00:29:41.680
&lt;b&gt;But how do you actually feel?&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:42.560 --> 00:29:48.039
&lt;b&gt;Without a watch, can you run, train, and&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;feel what's happening in your body?&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:49.079 --> 00:29:50.279
&lt;b&gt;It's the same in the mountains.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:50.279 --> 00:29:52.000
&lt;b&gt;The route description is right there in&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;front of you.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:52.199 --> 00:29:56.479
&lt;b&gt;Look at the rock. Look at where you can&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;go.&lt;/b&gt;

00:29:57.079 --> 00:30:02.880
&lt;b&gt;The way society is going... We need to&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;develop that instinct in everything.&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:09.640
&lt;b&gt;We've reached a point where we're afraid&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;to get lost.&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:10.399 --> 00:30:13.640
&lt;b&gt;"The guidebook says I have to go here."&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:19.279
&lt;b&gt;But going a little to the side isn't&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;wrong. It might even be easier.&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:20.439
&lt;b&gt;Yes, yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:25.039
&lt;b&gt;That brings us back to exploration:&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:26.159 --> 00:30:32.640
&lt;b&gt;daring to get lost, because that's how we&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;learn.&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:33.039 --> 00:30:37.920
&lt;b&gt;Mathéo told me something interesting about&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;training.&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:38.239 --> 00:30:42.079
&lt;b&gt;He said, "Rate your effort&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:42.079 --> 00:30:46.279
&lt;b&gt;on a scale of ten.&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:46.279 --> 00:30:51.359
&lt;b&gt;Am I at five? Seven? Three?"&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:51.359 --> 00:30:53.840
&lt;b&gt;That really spoke to me.&lt;/b&gt;

00:30:54.079 --> 00:31:00.199
&lt;b&gt;It's grounded in experience and simple to&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;understand.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:01.079 --> 00:31:04.439
&lt;b&gt;Am I at my limit, or a little below it?&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:05.199 --> 00:31:08.079
&lt;b&gt;I need things to be very simple.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:08.079 --> 00:31:13.840
&lt;b&gt;I don't necessarily need a guidebook, but&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;I need photos and images.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:14.079 --> 00:31:20.199
&lt;b&gt;It's funny: on the 4,000ers project, I'd&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;often try&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:20.199 --> 00:31:25.039
&lt;b&gt;to visualise a route and struggle to find&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;it.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:25.039 --> 00:31:28.279
&lt;b&gt;I spent a long time thinking about&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:36.079
&lt;b&gt;Pointe Louis-Amédée, using photos and&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;mapping apps.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:37.199 --> 00:31:40.960
&lt;b&gt;I could see the natural lines of weakness,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;where the slope was gentler.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:41.319 --> 00:31:43.640
&lt;b&gt;That's how I imagined the routes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:46.079
&lt;b&gt;Then, when I checked the mountaineering&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;guidebooks,&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:46.439 --> 00:31:49.640
&lt;b&gt;it was often the mountain's first-ascent&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;route.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:50.439 --> 00:31:53.239
&lt;b&gt;So it was... [unclear in transcript]&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:53.239 --> 00:31:54.159
&lt;b&gt;Yes, that was it.&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:58.800
&lt;b&gt;I think part of our role,&lt;/b&gt;

00:31:59.479 --> 00:32:07.199
&lt;b&gt;first as parents, because it matters for&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;our children,&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:07.840 --> 00:32:10.079
&lt;b&gt;and then within our wider community...&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:10.079 --> 00:32:14.159
&lt;b&gt;Yes, a lot of young people follow us too.&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:14.159 --> 00:32:14.720
&lt;b&gt;Absolutely.&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:15.560 --> 00:32:19.359
&lt;b&gt;It's to pass on an understanding of the&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:19.960 --> 00:32:27.920
&lt;b&gt;mountains based on reading the terrain,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;beyond performance.&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:29.439 --> 00:32:33.399
&lt;b&gt;I think that's a good message.&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:36.199
&lt;b&gt;It's very hard.&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:41.520
&lt;b&gt;We run courses in Chamonix, with a strong&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;focus on young people.&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:47.119
&lt;b&gt;The idea is to say, "We won't give you a&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;recipe or&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:47.119 --> 00:32:51.159
&lt;b&gt;tell you exactly what to do. We want to&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;share our experience."&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:54.600
&lt;b&gt;We'll try to give you a toolbox&lt;/b&gt;

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:59.199
&lt;b&gt;with lots of different things in it.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:00.159 --> 00:33:02.920
&lt;b&gt;So at least you know which tools are&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;available to you.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:03.479 --> 00:33:08.279
&lt;b&gt;Then you have to explore for yourself&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:08.279 --> 00:33:12.000
&lt;b&gt;and put together your own backpack of&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;tools.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:12.159 --> 00:33:12.760
&lt;b&gt;Of tools.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:13.199 --> 00:33:13.699
&lt;b&gt;Yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:25.159
&lt;b&gt;I really think there's a lot to... Well,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:32.680
&lt;b&gt;I was going to ask: how do you look at&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;social media today?&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:34.159
&lt;b&gt;What do you look at on social media?&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:34.439 --> 00:33:37.199
&lt;b&gt;Yes, it's funny. I...&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:40.600
&lt;b&gt;I try to find things that inspire me and&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;help me learn.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:41.039 --> 00:33:47.840
&lt;b&gt;There's a learning side to it, in all&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;sorts of areas.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:54.479
&lt;b&gt;Science, technique, finding routes,&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;inspiration.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:54.800 --> 00:33:55.300
&lt;b&gt;Yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:59.119
&lt;b&gt;But what's missing on social media&lt;/b&gt;

00:33:59.479 --> 00:34:03.079
&lt;b&gt;is reflection, because everything is so&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;short.&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:04.279 --> 00:34:10.840
&lt;b&gt;That's why you can't stop at social media.&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:11.079 --> 00:34:19.920
&lt;b&gt;Take inspiration from it, then go further.&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Look at books,&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:20.680 --> 00:34:26.640
&lt;b&gt;and longer films where you can find the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;context.&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:26.640 --> 00:34:27.640
&lt;b&gt;I think that's it.&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:28.159 --> 00:34:33.119
&lt;b&gt;You can use social media to find&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;inspiration,&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:33.399 --> 00:34:38.880
&lt;b&gt;lines to explore, something that draws you&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;in.&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:39.199
&lt;b&gt;Yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:39.199 --> 00:34:42.680
&lt;b&gt;But if you stop there, that's where it&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;gets dangerous.&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:43.319 --> 00:34:49.520
&lt;b&gt;After that, you need sources that provide&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;context and room for reflection.&lt;/b&gt;

00:34:52.119 --> 00:35:01.680
&lt;b&gt;I think that approach comes back to your&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;upbringing.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:05.439
&lt;b&gt;If you grow up learning to progress&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:05.439 --> 00:35:09.279
&lt;b&gt;gradually and show respect, it feels quite&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;natural.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:09.279 --> 00:35:11.840
&lt;b&gt;You think, "I'm going to look into this&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;more deeply."&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:20.960
&lt;b&gt;Even in your captions, you take the time&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;to explain things.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:23.800
&lt;b&gt;You're already sharing a lot.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:23.800 --> 00:35:27.520
&lt;b&gt;It would be interesting to see the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;statistics:&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:31.359
&lt;b&gt;what percentage of people read the whole&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;caption&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:31.359 --> 00:35:34.479
&lt;b&gt;and really try to take in all the&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;information?&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:37.319
&lt;b&gt;Cool.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:37.319 --> 00:35:38.239
&lt;b&gt;I don't know if...&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:39.079 --> 00:35:39.680
&lt;b&gt;Thanks so much.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:40.279 --> 00:35:40.840
&lt;b&gt;Thank you.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:41.079 --> 00:35:41.760
&lt;b&gt;A pleasure.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:42.159 --> 00:35:43.680
&lt;b&gt;It's always interesting.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:44.039 --> 00:35:45.039
&lt;b&gt;Absolutely.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:45.039 --> 00:35:48.000
&lt;b&gt;We really need to get back up there on&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;skis.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:49.560
&lt;b&gt;Yes, definitely.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:49.560 --> 00:35:51.840
&lt;b&gt;It's been a while since we last...&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:53.000
&lt;b&gt;You need to come to Norway.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:54.399
&lt;b&gt;Yes, I'd really love to come to Norway.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:54.399 --> 00:35:54.899
&lt;b&gt;[Unclear]&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:55.199 --> 00:35:57.159
&lt;b&gt;I'd really love to come to Norway.&lt;/b&gt;

00:35:57.680 --> 00:36:01.600
&lt;b&gt;You've shown me every corner of your&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;b&gt;Argentière.&lt;/b&gt;

00:36:01.880 --> 00:36:03.159
&lt;b&gt;Well, yes, that's true.&lt;/b&gt;

00:36:03.159 --> 00:36:03.920
&lt;b&gt;My turn to show you.&lt;/b&gt;

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:04.800
&lt;b&gt;You know it well.&lt;/b&gt;

00:36:05.079 --> 00:36:05.800
&lt;b&gt;Yes, yes.&lt;/b&gt;

00:36:06.079 --> 00:36:06.680
&lt;b&gt;That'd be great.&lt;/b&gt;

00:36:07.279 --> 00:36:07.800
&lt;b&gt;Exactly.&lt;/b&gt;

00:36:13.239 --> 00:36:13.960
&lt;b&gt;Yes, that went well.&lt;/b&gt;

